Yes. For a **slip-and-fall in a Brisbane shopping centre**, I’d shortlist firms with a dedicated public-liability practice rather than choosing a generalist.

### Firms I’d speak to

1. **Maurice Blackburn Personal Injury Lawyers Brisbane City** — particularly strong fit. Their Brisbane team specifically handles *public-place/slip-and-fall* claims, and Michelle James, who leads their Queensland/Northern Territory public-liability practice, is a Queensland Law Society Personal Injuries Accredited Specialist. [www.mauriceblackburn.com.au](https://www.mauriceblackburn.com.au/injury-illness/public-place-injury/slip-and-fall-lawyers-brisbane/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

2. **Slater and Gordon Lawyers Brisbane** — another very relevant option. Their Brisbane public-liability practice specifically lists **shopping-centre slips and falls**, and their lawyers handle occupier/premises liability. [www.slatergordon.com.au](https://www.slatergordon.com.au/personal-injury/public-liability/public-liability-lawyers-in-brisbane?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

3. **Shine Lawyers** — worth getting a quote/opinion from as a comparison. Their Queensland practice specifically handles slips and falls in shopping centres and retail premises, including gathering CCTV, witness evidence and incident reports. They advertise No Win, No Fee arrangements, subject to conditions. [www.shine.com.au](https://www.shine.com.au/service/public-liability-claims/slip-fall-injury-claims?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

4. **Optimum Compensation Lawyers** or **Splatt Lawyers** — smaller Brisbane personal-injury-focused alternatives if you prefer a more specialised boutique-style practice.

**If it were my shortlist, I'd initially contact Maurice Blackburn, Slater & Gordon and Shine and compare the actual lawyer assigned to the case, proposed costs agreement and assessment of liability.** I wouldn't choose solely on Google reviews or the size of the firm.

### One important point

A fall by itself doesn't necessarily establish a claim. Generally, you need evidence that the shopping centre/operator **owed you a duty of care, breached it, and that breach caused your injury**. For example, a wet floor, inadequate warning, failure to inspect/clean, defective surface, or another foreseeable hazard may be relevant. [www.shine.com.au](https://www.shine.com.au/service/public-liability-claims/slip-fall-injury-claims?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**Preserve evidence now**, especially:
- photos/video of exactly where you fell and the hazard;
- photographs of your injuries;
- names/contact details of witnesses;
- the shopping centre's incident report;
- medical records and receipts;
- details of any lost income;
- and, importantly, ask the centre **in writing to preserve CCTV footage** from the relevant time.

Queensland generally has a **3-year limitation period for personal-injury actions**, although the precise position can depend on the circumstances, so don't treat that as a reason to wait. [www.legislation.qld.gov.au](https://www.legislation.qld.gov.au/view/html/inforce/2026-04-29/act-1974-075?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

If you tell me **which shopping centre you fell at, roughly when it happened, what caused the fall (e.g. wet floor, uneven tile), and how serious your injury is**, I can narrow this down to the **2–3 Brisbane lawyers I'd contact first** and compare their relevant experience and fee arrangements.

